    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "A Heart That Grieves Over Sin and a Grace That Shuts the Door",
      "reflection": "One of the most sobering and intimate revelations of God's heart in all of Scripture occurs in Genesis 6:5–6. The Lord looks upon the earth and sees that 'every imagination of the thoughts of man’s heart was only evil continually.' And how does God react? He does not laugh in cruel detachment. Verse 6 records: 'Yahweh was sorry that he had made man on the earth, and it grieved him in his heart.' God feels the pain of human rebellion. Sin breaks the heart of God because He is holy and because He loves His creation.\n\nYet directly against the dark canvas of universal depravity shines one solitary diamond: 'But Noah found favor in Yahweh’s eyes' (Genesis 6:8). Notice the order! Noah did not earn God's favor by his spotless perfection; he found favor (chen, unmerited grace), and that grace empowered him to live righteously in a crooked generation.\n\nLook also at the tenderness of God's invitation in Genesis 7:1. God does not tell Noah, 'Go into the ark.' He says, 'Come into the ship.' When you tell someone to 'come,' it means you are already inside waiting for them! God went into that refuge with Noah. And when the animals and the family were safely aboard, Genesis 7:16 gives us this unforgettable detail: 'and Yahweh shut him in.' Noah did not have to worry about sealing the heavy timber door against the battering waves; the loving hand of God sealed them in safety. When you are in Christ, you are sealed by a divine hand that no storm can pry open."
    },
