    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "God Remembers, and Sets His War-Bow in the Sky",
      "reflection": "Notice the breathtaking turning point at the very opening of Genesis 8: 'God remembered Noah, all the animals, and all the livestock that were with him in the ship' (Genesis 8:1). When Scripture says God 'remembered' someone, it does not mean He had suffered a lapse of memory and suddenly woke up. In biblical language, God's remembering signifies His active, covenant faithfulness moving into decisive action to rescue and deliver. For months Noah had heard nothing but the roar of crushing waves and the crying of animals in the dark hull. But God had never taken His eyes off that wooden chest.\n\nWhen Noah finally steps onto dry land, look at his first priority. He does not rush to claim real estate or build a mansion for his family. He builds an altar to Yahweh and offers precious clean animals in grateful worship. And God smells the soothing aroma of that sacrifice, speaking a promise of staggering mercy: He will never again curse the earth with a flood, even though human hearts remain fragile and flawed (Genesis 8:21).\n\nThen God gives the sign of this covenant: the rainbow in the rain cloud (Genesis 9:13). In ancient times, a warrior's bow was an instrument of death, aimed at an enemy to shoot lethal arrows. But notice where God hangs His bow! The rainbow is unstrung, resting in the clouds, and its arch is pointed upward toward heaven, not downward at humanity. God effectively says: 'I have hung up My war-bow. Before another universal flood strikes the earth, the arrow of divine judgment would have to strike My own heart.' And at the Cross of Calvary, that is precisely what took place: Jesus Christ absorbed the arrow of God's wrath so that you and I could walk under the eternal rainbow of His peace."
    },
