    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Does God Deserve Worship Only When the Sun Shines?",
      "reflection": "The cosmic drama of the book of Job hinges on a sneering question posed by Satan to the Almighty: 'Does Job fear God for nothing?' (Job 1:9). The adversary argued that genuine, unselfish devotion does not exist. He claimed that Job was only a mercenary believer—someone who worshiped God because God paid him well with flocks, herds, healthy children, and a protective hedge. Satan was essentially arguing: 'Strip away his blessings, and he will curse You to Your face.'\n\nWhen wave after wave of catastrophic loss crashes over Job—wiping out his livelihood and crushing all ten of his beloved children in a single hour—heaven and hell leaned forward to see what would emerge from his heart. Would he curse God? Would he throw away his faith? Verse 20 provides one of the most majestic moments in human history: 'Then Job arose, and tore his robe, and shaved his head, and fell down on the ground, and worshiped.'\n\nNotice that Job did not pretend it didn't hurt. He tore his robe and shaved his head in raw, honest agony. Tears flowed freely. True faith does not suppress grief; it carries its shattered pieces straight into the throne room of God and whispers through trembling lips: 'The Lord gave, and the Lord has taken away. Blessed be the name of the Lord.' Job worshiped God not because of what was in his hands, but because of who sat upon the throne. God is worthy of our worship on the darkest day of loss just as He is on the brightest day of abundance."
    },
