    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "I Am Not Your Accused; I Am Your Majestic Creator",
      "reflection": "For thirty-five chapters, Job sat in the dirt asking 'Why?'. He demanded an explanation. He longed to present his case before God as in a courtroom, confident that he had not committed the sins his friends accused him of. And then, in Job 38, God finally shows up in a roaring whirlwind. But notice what God does not do: He does not answer a single one of Job's 'why' questions! He never even mentions Satan or the cosmic challenge in chapters 1 and 2.\n\nInstead, God takes Job on a breathtaking, mind-boggling tour of the universe. 'Where were you when I laid the foundations of the earth? Who told the oceans, \"Thus far you shall come, and no farther\"? Can you lead forth the constellations or feed the hungry young ravens?' For four chapters, God asks question after question, revealing that the universe is infinitely larger, more interconnected, and more wisely governed than human intellect can fathom.\n\nGod was not bullying Job; He was curing Job's myopic anxiety with the antidote of divine majesty. He was saying: 'Job, if I have the wisdom and power to balance the stars, command the lightning, and feed the eagle on the crag, can you not trust Me with your fragile life, even when you cannot see the full picture?'\n\nAnd look at Job's glorious response in Job 42:5–6. He does not say, 'Now I understand all my questions.' He says, 'I had heard of You by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees You.' The presence of God was the answer! When you truly behold the majesty of God, the questions that kept you awake at night do not necessarily get answered—they get dissolved in worship. And when did God restore Job's fortunes? Job 42:10 says: 'Yahweh restored the fortunes of Job, when he prayed for his friends.' Forgiving and interceding for the very people who bruised your soul unleashes the double-portion restoration of heaven."
    },
