    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Midnight Stargazer and the God Who Walks the Path Alone",
      "reflection": "Genesis 15:6 is one of the most foundational hinges in the entire Bible: 'He believed in Yahweh, and he credited it to him for righteousness.' Notice where Abram was when this occurred. He was not in a temple; he had not performed a single ritual; he was not circumcised (that comes two chapters later in Genesis 17); and Moses' law would not exist for another four centuries. Abram was simply an old, childless man standing under the cold desert sky, looking up at thousands of glittering stars, and deciding that God was trustworthy. God took that simple, childlike faith and credited it to Abram's ledger as spotless righteousness.\n\nThen look at the covenant ceremony in verses 9–18. In the ancient Near East, when two parties entered into a blood covenant, they would butcher animals, lay the halves opposite each other, and walk between the bloody pieces together. The ritual meant: 'If I break my oath, may I be ripped apart like these animals.' But when the sun went down and deep darkness fell on Abram, God put Abram into a deep sleep! Abram did not walk between the pieces.\n\nInstead, what passed between the severed animals? A smoking firepot and a blazing torch—the manifest presence of God Almighty! God walked the bloody path alone. God was taking an unconditional oath: 'Abram, if this covenant is broken, the curse will fall on Me.' And two thousand years later, on a dark Friday outside Jerusalem's walls, God took the curse of that covenant upon His own flesh at Calvary so that we who believe might be justified forever."
    },
