    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "El Shaddai: Redefining Identity When Human Strength Ends",
      "reflection": "For thirteen long years between Genesis 16 and 17, Scripture records no vision, no altar, and no spoken word from God to Abram. Abram had settled into life with teenage Ishmael, assuming that this was the fulfillment of God's promise. But when Abram reached ninety-nine years of age, God shattered the silence with a thunderous revelation: 'I am El Shaddai—God Almighty. Walk before Me, and be blameless' (Genesis 17:1).\n\nWhy did God wait until Abram was ninety-nine and Sarah's womb was biologically dead? Because God wanted to ensure that when the child arrived, nobody could claim it was the result of human stamina, vitamins, or clever surrogacy. It had to be the pure, supernatural life of God! And then God does something staggering: He renames them. He changes Abram ('Exalted Father') to Abraham ('Father of a Multitude'). Imagine the awkwardness! For the next year, whenever Abraham introduced himself in Canaanite marketplaces, people were calling a childless centenarian 'Father of Multitudes.' Faith speaks God's promise before the physical reality is visible.\n\nAnd when the heavenly visitors arrive at Mamre in Genesis 18, Sarah chuckles cynically behind the tent flap: 'At my age? With my withered body?' But God's reply echoes across human history down to your exact circumstances today: 'Is anything too hard for Yahweh?' (Genesis 18:14). In Hebrew, the word for 'too hard' (pala) means 'too extraordinary, too miraculous, too wonderful.' Nothing is beyond the reach of the God who calls life out of graves."
    },
