    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "On the Mountain of the Lord It Shall Be Provided",
      "reflection": "Genesis 22 is holy ground. God had promised Abraham that Isaac was the seed through whom all nations would be blessed. And now God says: 'Take your son, your only son, whom you love, and offer him.' Why would God ask for the very promise He gave? Because God was testing whether Abraham loved the gift more than the Giver. An idol is anything—even a God-given blessing, child, or ministry—that takes first place before God Himself.\n\nLook at Abraham's staggering faith. In verse 5, he tells the servants: 'I and the boy will go yonder and worship, and we will come again to you.' Notice the plural! Hebrews 11:19 explains what was happening in Abraham's mind: he reasoned that even if he reduced Isaac to ashes on that altar, God had the power to raise him from the dead to keep His promise!\n\nAnd behold the breathtaking prophetic picture of the Cross! Isaac, the beloved son, carried the heavy wood of the sacrifice up the mountain on his own back—just as Jesus carried the wooden beam of the Cross up the hill of Calvary. Isaac willingly submitted his wrists to be bound—just as Jesus willingly laid down His life. And at the climactic moment, when heaven stopped the blade, a ram was found caught by its horns in the thicket. An innocent substitute died so that the son could go free!\n\nAbraham named that spot Yahweh-Yireh: 'The Lord will provide.' In Hebrew, Yireh is linked to vision: 'The Lord will see to it.' And two thousand years later, on that very same mountain range outside Jerusalem, God the Father did not spare His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all (Romans 8:32). Whatever you place on the altar of surrender, God meets you there with His all-sufficient provision."
    },
