    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Being on the Way, the Lord Led Me",
      "reflection": "The journey of Abraham's servant in Genesis 24 is one of the most practical and inspiring case studies of prayer and divine guidance in the entire Bible. Eliezer faced a daunting task: find a godly, willing wife for Isaac in a distant land without taking Isaac out of the Promised Land. How did he tackle this heavy vocational responsibility? He did not rely on clever marketing or human charisma. He knelt down beside the city well and talked directly to God.\n\nNotice the nature of his prayer in Genesis 24:12–14. He prayed with humility ('show kindness to my master'), he prayed with specificity, and he prayed for character! He did not ask for a maiden who was merely wealthy or stunning; he asked for a woman with a servant's heart—someone willing to draw water not just for a stranger, but for ten thirsty camels! Drawing water for ten camels meant hauling hundreds of pounds of water from the bottom of a stone well. Rebekah's immediate, joyful willingness revealed her inner character.\n\nAnd look at verse 15: 'Before he had finished speaking, behold, Rebekah came out.' God answered before the prayer even finished echoing off Eliezer's lips! But the most beautiful verse is Eliezer's testimony in verse 27: 'As for me, being on the way, Yahweh led me.' God directs moving feet. When you step out in faithful obedience and acknowledge God in all your ways, He straightens your path and leads you straight to His divine appointments."
    },
