    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Rehoboth: When You Yield, God Makes Room",
      "reflection": "Isaac is often overshadowed between his towering father Abraham and his dramatic son Jacob. Yet Isaac's life displays a quiet, heroic strength that our combative world desperately needs to learn: the power of non-retaliation and peace. In Genesis 26, famine struck the land. While everyone else assumed survival required fleeing to the lush Nile Delta of Egypt, God told Isaac to stay put in dry Gerar. Isaac obeyed, sowed seeds in a drought, and reaped a staggering hundredfold harvest! God can bless you in a famine when you obey His voice.\n\nBut divine blessing often attracts worldly envy. The Philistines grew jealous, stopped up his father's wells with dirt, and King Abimelech demanded that he leave. Isaac packed up his tents and moved into the valley. His servants dug and struck a gushing spring of living water. The Philistines arrived claiming: 'The water is ours!' What did Isaac do? He did not pull out swords. He named the well Esek ('Contention') and walked away. He dug another well; they fought over that one too. He named it Sitnah ('Enmity') and walked away again.\n\nTo worldly eyes, Isaac looked like a pushover. But in God's eyes, Isaac was a giant of faith. He refused to waste his life brawling over muddy holes when the Creator of all water was on his side! He dug a third well, and this time there was no strife. He named it Rehoboth: 'For now Yahweh has made room for us, and we will be fruitful in the land' (Genesis 26:22). And notice what happened next: his enemies showed up at his tent in Beersheba, humbled and asking for peace, saying: 'We saw plainly that Yahweh was with you' (v. 28). Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall inherit the earth."
    },
