    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "God's Sovereign Covenant Outlasts Our Manipulative Messes",
      "reflection": "Genesis 27 is a painful picture of a dysfunctional covenant home. Isaac was attempting to bless his favorite hunter Esau in defiance of God's prenatal prophecy that the elder would serve the younger (Genesis 25:23). Rebekah, instead of praying and trusting God to handle blind Isaac, concocted a fraudulent plot of skins and deception. And Jacob, whose very name meant 'Heel-catcher' or 'Deceiver,' lied directly to his aged father, even invoking God's holy name to seal his lie: 'Yahweh your God gave me success' (v. 20).\n\nHuman scheming produced immediate disaster: a shattered home, a murderous brother, a weeping father, and a lonely exile. Jacob had to run for his life with only a staff in his hand. Yet look at the scandalous grace of Genesis 28! When Jacob lays his head upon a cold stone in the desert night, fleeing like a fugitive, heaven does not open with thunderbolts of wrath. Heaven opens with a stairway! The angels of God ascend and descend, and Yahweh stands at the top, speaking unconditional grace: 'Behold, I am with you, and will keep you wherever you go... I will not leave you until I have done that which I have spoken of to you' (Genesis 28:15).\n\nGod does not endorse Jacob's deceit—Jacob would spend the next twenty years being disciplined under the master-schemer Laban. But God's covenant is not fueled by human perfection; it is anchored in His sovereign mercy. God pursues the runaway deceiver in the dark and pledges never to leave him."
    },
