    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "This Time I Will Praise Yahweh: Finding Worth in God Alone",
      "reflection": "The emotional heartache of Leah is palpable in Genesis 29. She was the unchosen, second-tier sister. She was given to Jacob under the cover of darkness as a pawn in her father's greedy schemes. When morning light came, Jacob looked at her with disappointment and disgust, running to Laban to demand Rachel. Verse 31 states with heartbreaking bluntness: 'Yahweh saw that Leah was hated [unloved].' But look at what follows: 'and He opened her womb.' God sees the overlooked. God defends the unloved.\n\nListen to the raw ache in Leah's baby-naming journey. When her first son was born, she named him Reuben: 'Now my husband will love me!' When her second was born, she named him Simeon: 'Yahweh heard that I was hated.' When her third was born, she named him Levi: 'Now my husband will be joined to me!' For years, Leah was on an exhausting emotional treadmill, trying to use her fertility to force her husband to give her the affection she craved. She was measuring her self-worth through the fickle eyes of a man.\n\nAnd then, in verse 35, she conceives a fourth son. Look at the miraculous shift in her language! She mentions Jacob's name zero times. She does not mention her husband, her loneliness, or her desire for validation. She lifts her eyes to heaven and proclaims: 'This time I will praise Yahweh!' She named him Judah ('Praise'). She stepped off the treadmill of seeking human approval and anchored her soul in the worship of the God who loved her. And what was God's reward? When Jacob died, he was not buried beside Rachel in Bethlehem; he requested to be buried in the family tomb of Machpelah beside Leah! And through Judah, Leah gave the world the King of kings, Jesus Christ."
    },
