    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Buried Under the Oak: Returning to the First Altar",
      "reflection": "Thirty years after fleeing from Esau, Jacob was in danger of spiritual drift. He had settled down outside the city of Shechem, bought land, and allowed his household to accumulate pagan household idols, good-luck charms, and Mesopotamian trinkets. The tragic moral collapse of Genesis 34—the violation of Dinah and the bloodbath caused by Simeon and Levi—woke Jacob up to the terrifying reality that his home was spiritually compromised.\n\nAnd into that crisis, God's voice broke like a clarion trumpet: 'Arise, go up to Bethel, and live there. Make there an altar to God, who appeared to you when you fled...' (Genesis 35:1). God was calling Jacob back to his first love! Thirty years earlier, Jacob had vowed at Bethel that Yahweh would be his God. Now it was time to fulfill that vow.\n\nLook at Jacob's decisive leadership in verses 2–4. He told his family: 'Put away the foreign gods, purify yourselves, and change your clothes.' True revival is not an emotional surge; it is a practical housecleaning! They gathered every pagan amulet and silver figurine, and Jacob dug a hole and buried them deep under the great oak tree at Shechem. He buried the past. And when they journeyed to Bethel, a supernatural terror from God fell upon all the surrounding pagan cities, shielding them from harm.\n\nAt Bethel, Jacob built the altar and called it El-Bethel ('The God of the House of God'). He wasn't in love with the place; he was in love with the God of the place! Centuries later, in Joshua 24, Joshua gathered the entire nation of Israel at that exact same oak tree in Shechem to echo Jacob's cry: 'Put away your foreign gods... as for me and my house, we will serve Yahweh.' Is there something in your life that needs to be buried under the oak today?"
    },
