    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "One More Night with the Frogs: The Anatomy of a Hard Heart",
      "reflection": "The ten plagues of Egypt were not random ecological disasters; they were targeted divine judgments against the pantheon of Egyptian gods (Numbers 33:4). The Nile River was worshipped as the god Hapi, the source of life; God turned it into stinking, undrinkable blood. The frog was revered as the goddess Heqet, symbol of fertility; God overwhelmed Egypt with millions of frogs crawling into their beds, kneading bowls, and cooking ovens. God systematically demonstrated that the gods of this world are utterly helpless before Yahweh.\n\nYet the most tragic character in this drama is Pharaoh himself. Look at Exodus 8:9-10. When the frogs were driving him mad, Pharaoh begged Moses to pray for relief. Moses gave him the privilege of naming the hour: 'When shall I entreat God for you?' Think about that question! If your bedroom, kitchen, and clothes were crawling with slimy frogs, you would shout: 'Right now! This very second!' But Pharaoh astonishingly replied: 'Tomorrow.' He preferred to spend one more night with the frogs rather than humble himself before the God of Israel today!\n\nThis is the tragedy of procrastination and the hardening of the human heart. Whenever God brings conviction, pride says, 'Tomorrow. Next week. Later.' Notice verse 15: 'When Pharaoh saw that there was a respite, he hardened his heart.' When the crisis passed and breathing room returned, Pharaoh reverted to his old rebellion. Guard your heart: do not treat God's patience as permission to persist in sin. When God speaks today, do not harden your heart."
    },
