    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "When I See the Blood: The Token of Atoning Grace",
      "reflection": "Exodus 12 is the beating heart of Old Testament redemptive theology. When the destroying angel swept through Egypt at midnight, he was not looking at the moral resume, cultural background, or good intentions of the people inside the houses. He was not checking whether the inhabitants were educated, wealthy, or naturally gentle. He was looking for one thing and one thing alone: **the blood on the doorposts!**\n\nNotice the precise wording of Exodus 12:13: 'When I see the blood, I will pass over you.' It does not say, 'When you understand all theology,' or 'When you feel strong faith,' or 'When your character is flawless.' It says: 'When I see the blood!' Our salvation rests on God’s gaze upon the sacrifice, not on the strength of our feelings. The youngest child, the weakest elder, and the most nervous Hebrew who huddled behind that blood-stained door was 100% safe—not because of their personal strength, but because of the blood of the unblemished lamb.\n\nEvery single detail of that sacred night pointed forward to Jesus Christ. Paul wrote in 1 Corinthians 5:7: 'For indeed, Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place.' Jesus is the Lamb without blemish. His blood was shed at twilight on Calvary. And when by faith we apply His blood to the doorposts of our hearts, the judgment of God passes over us forever. You are not saved by your own good works; you are saved by the precious blood of the Lamb."
    },
