    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Song of the Shore: Turning Deliverance into Doxology",
      "reflection": "Exodus 15 contains the very first recorded song in all of human Scripture. Think about the significance of that! The Bible does not open with a song of creation, nor a song of Abraham's journey, nor a song of Joseph's palace. The first song in the Bible is a **song of redemption**! When you stand on the far shore of the Red Sea, look back across the churning waters, and see that the tyrannical master who beat you, oppressed your children, and hunted your life has been permanently swallowed up by the judgment of God—the only appropriate response is worship.\n\nNotice how the song begins: 'Yah is my strength and song. He has become my salvation.' (Exodus 15:2). In the middle of the battle, God is our strength. But when the battle is won, that strength turns into a song! God does not merely want us to endure trials in stoic silence; He wants to fill our mouths with joyful praise. And look at Miriam, now an elderly woman around ninety years old, who eighty years earlier had stood by the Nile's reeds watching baby Moses. She grabs her tambourine, and all the women follow her with dancing, singing: 'Sing to Yahweh, for he has triumphed gloriously!'\n\nNotice also Psalm 106:12: 'Then they believed his words. They sang his praise.' The tragedy, as the Psalmist laments, is that Israel quickly forgot this song when they reached the desert. But Revelation 15:3 tells us that at the end of human history, the saints standing on the sea of glass mingled with fire will sing the exact same anthem: 'the song of Moses the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb!' True worship is eternal; let the song of your deliverance begin today."
    },
