    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Sacredness of Secular Work: Anointed for Craftsmanship",
      "reflection": "Ask the average believer who was the first person in Scripture said to be 'filled with the Spirit of God,' and they will likely guess a prophet like Moses, an apostle like Peter, or a king like David. But look at Exodus 31:2-3! The very first human being in the entire Bible explicitly described as filled with the Spirit of God is **Bezalel**—a blue-collar craftsman who worked with metal, wood, textiles, and precious stones!\n\nThis is a radical biblical truth that tears down our false division between 'sacred ministry' and 'secular work.' In God’s kingdom, carving wood, soldering metal, coding software, designing graphics, bookkeeping, nursing, and sweeping floors can be just as Spirit-filled as preaching a sermon or leading a prayer meeting! Notice what the Spirit gave Bezalel: 'wisdom, understanding, knowledge, and all kinds of workmanship' (v. 3). The Holy Spirit cares about engineering, aesthetics, ergonomics, and structural integrity because our God is the supreme Architect and Creator of the cosmos.\n\nNotice also Colossians 3:23-24: 'Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart, as working for the Lord, and not for men, knowing that from the Lord you will receive the reward... for you serve the Lord Christ!' God does not measure the sacredness of your work by whether it happens inside a church building, but by **whom you are doing it for**. When you hammer nails, write code, care for children, or manage spreadsheets with excellence and integrity for Christ's glory, your daily work becomes a holy altar of worship."
    },
