    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Severity of Strange Fire: Reverence in the Presence of the Holy",
      "reflection": "Leviticus 10 is one of the most sobering, jarring chapters in all of Scripture. Only moments after the glorious fire of God consumed the sacrifice in chapter 9, bringing the whole nation to their knees in worship, catastrophe struck. Nadab and Abihu—young, privileged priests who had seen God's sapphire pavement on Sinai—took their censers, ignited their own fire, added incense, and rushed into the sanctuary offering 'strange fire before Yahweh, which He had not commanded them' (v. 1).\n\nInstantly, the same fire that had consumed the sacrifice consumed them! Why such severe judgment? Because God is not a casual mascot. God had explicitly commanded that incense was to be lit exclusively with coals from the bronze altar of sacrifice, where atoning blood had been sprinkled. Nadab and Abihu bypassed the atoning blood and manufactured their own fire! They treated the holy as common. They substituted human enthusiasm, novelty, and convenience for God's clear revelation.\n\nNotice Moses' profound words to grieving Aaron in verse 3: 'This is what Yahweh spoke of, saying: \"I will show myself holy to those who come near me, and before all the people I will be glorified.\"' Notice that phrase: **'to those who come near me.'** Greater spiritual proximity brings greater accountability! If you are close to God, you cannot play games with holiness. And look at Aaron's response: 'Aaron held his peace.' He did not shake his fist at God; in agonized silence, he acknowledged that God is righteous.\n\nHebrews 12:28-29 warns New Testament believers: 'Let us offer service acceptably to God with reverence and awe, for our God is a consuming fire!' Grace does not make God less holy; grace gives us the reverent awe to worship Him according to His truth."
    },
