    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Two Goats of Calvary: Propitiation and Expiation",
      "reflection": "Leviticus 16 gives us the most comprehensive visual portrait of the Gospel in the Old Testament. On Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement, the High Priest took **two goats**. Why two? Because no single animal could adequately represent what Jesus Christ would accomplish on the cross! Together, the two goats illustrate the twin realities of redemption: **propitiation** (the satisfying of God's holy justice) and **expiation** (the removal of our guilt and sin).\n\nThe first goat was slaughtered as a sin offering. Its blood was carried behind the veil into the pitch-black Holy of Holies and sprinkled upon the golden Mercy Seat directly over the broken law inside the Ark. God’s holy justice looked down, saw the blood of the innocent substitute, and was satisfied! The penalty of death was fully paid.\n\nThen look at the second goat: the **Scapegoat** (*Azazel*). Aaron laid both hands firmly on the head of the live goat and confessed over it all the iniquities, transgressions, and rebellious sins of Israel. All the moral filth of the nation was symbolically transferred to the head of this innocent goat. Then a man led the goat far out into the barren, uninhabited wilderness and let it run free into the desert horizon, never to be seen again! Psalm 103:12 sings of this: 'As far as the east is from the west, so far has He removed our transgressions from us!'\n\nHebrews 9:12 proclaims that Jesus did not enter with the blood of goats, but with His own blood, entering heaven itself once for all to obtain eternal redemption! On the cross, Jesus was the first goat: His blood satisfied the righteous wrath of God against our sin. And Jesus was the scapegoat: He bore our sins outside the camp, carrying our guilt into the oblivion of God's forgetfulness. You are completely clean before Yahweh!"
    },
