    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Silent Prophet and the Vindicating God: The Armor of Meekness",
      "reflection": "Numbers 12 exposes the venomous power of the tongue when infected by jealousy. Miriam was a prophetess who had led the women in worship at the Red Sea; Aaron was the consecrated High Priest. Yet they became deeply envious of their younger brother's supreme spiritual influence. Notice their tactic: they attacked Moses' personal life—his marriage to a dark-skinned Cushite woman—to mask their real grievance: 'Has Yahweh indeed spoken only with Moses? Hasn't He spoken with us as well?' (v. 2). Criticism of someone's personal life is frequently a smokescreen for envy of their spiritual influence.\n\nAnd look at Moses' reaction in verse 3: **he said nothing!** 'Now the man Moses was very humble, more than all the men who were on the surface of the earth.' Moses did not convene a press conference, pull rank, or fire back insults. He remained completely silent. Why? Because Moses knew that when you belong to God, you don't have to defend your own reputation! If you defend yourself, God steps back; but if you hold your peace in humble meekness, God Himself steps forward to defend you!\n\nNotice verse 2: 'And Yahweh heard it.' God hears every whispered slander, every critical gossip session behind closed doors. Suddenly, God called all three siblings to the Tent of Meeting, stepped out of the cloud, and rebuked Miriam and Aaron with terrifying clarity: 'Moses is faithful in all My house... Why then were you not afraid to speak against My servant?' And when Miriam was struck white with leprosy, look at Moses' Christlike heart: he didn't say, 'Serves you right!' He cried out in agony: 'Heal her, God, I beg You!' That is true meekness: praying mercy over the very people who slandered you."
    },
