    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Standing in the Breach: The Power of an Intercessor's Heart",
      "reflection": "Psalm 106:23 uses an unforgettable architectural metaphor to describe Moses: 'Therefore He said that He would destroy them, had not Moses His chosen stood before Him in the breach, to turn away His wrath!' In ancient warfare, when battering rams smashed a hole through the stone wall of a city, that gaping hole was called 'the breach.' The bravest warrior in the army would throw himself directly into the gap, standing between the invading enemy and his defenseless people. Moses stood in the breach between God's righteous wrath and rebellious Israel!\n\nNotice the supreme temptation Moses faced in Numbers 14:12. God said: 'I will destroy them with pestilence, and I will make of you a nation greater and mightier than they!' Think of how enticing that offer was to fallen human nature. If Moses had agreed, his name would have replaced Abraham's, Isaac's, and Jacob's! History would speak not of the God of Abraham, but of the God of Moses! But Moses didn't care about his name; he cared about **God's Name**!\n\nListen to Moses' argument: 'Lord, if You destroy them here, the pagan nations will say, \"Yahweh was not able to bring them into the land!\"' Moses was passionately jealous for the glory of God. And then he quoted God's own words back to Him from Exodus 34: 'Yahweh is slow to anger, and abundant in loving kindness... Pardon, I beg you!' (vv. 18-19). And God answered: 'I have pardoned according to your word!' (v. 20). God invites His intercessors into the gap. Jesus Christ is the ultimate Intercessor who stepped into the breach on Calvary, taking the crushing blow of God's holy wrath so that we might be pardoned forever."
    },
