    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Tragedy of Misrepresenting God: Why Striking the Rock Cost Moses Canaan",
      "reflection": "Numbers 20 is one of the most sobering warnings for Christian leaders in the entire Bible. Moses had served God faithfully for forty exhausting years. He had endured mutinies, slander, hardships, and the death of his sister Miriam. Yet in the fortieth year, at the finish line of the wilderness journey, Moses committed an error that cost him entrance into the Promised Land.\n\nWhat was Moses' sin? At first glance, hitting a rock twice instead of speaking to it might seem like a trivial technicality. But look closely at what happened. First, **Moses lost his temper and spoke rashly**: Psalm 106:33 says, 'They were rebellious against his spirit, and he spoke rashly with his lips.' Moses yelled: 'Hear now, you rebels! Shall WE bring water out of this rock for you?' (v. 10). Notice the word 'we'! Moses took credit alongside Aaron as if they were producing the water, stealing God's glory.\n\nSecond, **Moses misrepresented God's character**. In Numbers 20, God was not furious with Israel; God did not rebuke them for their thirst. God wanted to show them gentle, tender, overflowing grace. But by storming before the rock in red-faced fury, shouting insults, and violently striking the rock twice, Moses gave Israel the impression that God was an irritable, furious tyrant!\n\nThird, **Moses broke the sacred theological type of Christ**! In Exodus 17 at Horeb, God commanded Moses to strike the rock once, typifying Christ being struck once for all on Calvary. Now at Kadesh, the rock was only to be *spoken to*, typifying that because Christ was already struck on the cross, we only need to ask in faith to receive living water. By striking the rock twice, Moses symbolically re-crucified the Rock! God said: 'Because you did not sanctify Me in the eyes of Israel, you shall not bring them in.' God takes how we represent His holy character with the utmost seriousness."
    },
