    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Scarlet Cord in the Window: Grace for the Outcast",
      "reflection": "Rahab is one of the most stunning trophies of divine grace in all of world literature. Look at her background: She was a **Canaanite**—a member of a corrupt, pagan culture under divine judgment. And she was a **prostitute**—a woman whose life was marked by brokenness, shame, and social stigma. If ancient religious legalists were drawing up a list of candidates for God's mercy, Rahab would have been at the absolute bottom.\n\nYet look at her faith! In Joshua 2:11, this pagan woman spoke words of theological brilliance that outshone the ten unbelieving Hebrew spies at Kadesh: 'For Yahweh your God, He is God in heaven above and on earth beneath!' While the mighty king of Jericho and his warriors were paralyzed in terror, Rahab made a decisive, courageous choice: she renounced her doomed city and threw herself on the mercy of the living God!\n\nAnd look at the token of her salvation: **a scarlet cord tied in her window!** Just as the blood of the Passover lamb on the doorposts marked Hebrew homes for salvation in Egypt, that crimson cord dangling from the wall of Jericho marked Rahab's house for salvation in Canaan! When the trumpets blasted and the megalithic stone walls of Jericho came crashing down in rubble, one section of the wall remained standing like a solitary tower of mercy: the house with the scarlet cord!\n\nEven more breathtaking is what God did with Rahab's future. God did not relegate her to second-class status. She was welcomed into the covenant community of Israel, married Salmon (a prince of Judah), gave birth to godly Boaz, became the great-great-grandmother of King David, and is named in Matthew 1:5 in the royal genealogy of Jesus Christ! James 2:25 celebrates her works of faith, and Hebrews 11:31 enshrines her in the Hall of Faith. No matter how broken, messy, or stained your past may be, the scarlet blood of Christ can redeem your life and write you into God's royal family!"
    },
