    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Wet Feet Before Dry Ground: Stepping into the Impossible",
      "reflection": "Look at the timing of the Jordan crossing in Joshua 3:15: 'The Jordan overflows all its banks all the time of harvest.' God did not lead Israel to the Jordan during the dry summer months when the river was a sluggish, knee-deep stream. God brought them to the riverbank during the spring harvest runoff, when melting snow from Mount Hermon turned the Jordan into a raging, torrential, muddy brown flood over a mile wide! To human eyes, crossing was suicidal.\n\nNow notice the divine requirement in verse 13: God told the priests carrying the heavy Ark of the Covenant: 'When the soles of your feet rest in the waters, the waters shall be cut off.' Notice the order: **God did not part the waters while the priests were sitting comfortably in their tents!** The priests had to march forward, carry the Ark on their shoulders, and step directly into the roaring floodwater! It was only when their sandals got wet—only when they stepped into the edge of the river in radical obedience—that the roaring current halted thirty miles upstream at the city of Adam!\n\nFaith is not waiting until all problems vanish before you obey. Faith is stepping forward in obedience while the river is still flooding, knowing that the God who commands you will hold back the waters. And look at what Joshua did in Joshua 4: he took twelve heavy stones from the dry riverbed and built an altar at Gilgal. Why? Because human beings are chronically prone to forgetfulness! When life is hard, we forget God's past miracles. Joshua said: 'When your children ask in time to come, \"What mean these stones?\" tell them: Israel came over this Jordan on dry land!' Build memorial stones of thanksgiving in your heart so your faith never forgets God's mighty hand."
    },
