    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "The Moldy Bread Test: When Common Sense Bypasses Prayer",
      "reflection": "Joshua 9 exposes one of the most subtle traps in the spiritual life. Notice how the enemy came against Israel: In chapter 6 at Jericho, the enemy was a towering wall. In chapter 7 at Ai, the enemy was a fierce counter-attack. But in chapter 9, the enemy did not come with chariots, swords, or threatening shouts. The enemy arrived as humble, weary travelers wearing dusty rags, limping in patched sandals, offering dry, moldy bread, and speaking pious words about the glory of Yahweh!\n\nLook at verse 14—one of the most searching sentences in the Old Testament: 'The men sampled their provisions, and didn’t ask counsel from Yahweh’s mouth.' Joshua and the leaders used their eyes: the bread looked moldy. They used their hands: the wineskins felt cracked. They used their tongues: the crumbs tasted dry. They used their human logic: everything seemed to match the travelers' story. But they made one catastrophic omission: **they didn't pray!** They relied on their own sensory common sense instead of seeking God's counsel. When an opportunity looks obvious and sensible, that is precisely when we must hit our knees and inquire of the Lord.\n\nYet notice the inspiring sequel in verses 19-20. When the deception was discovered three days later, the leaders did not tear up the contract or slaughter the Gibeonites. They said: 'We have sworn to them by Yahweh, and we will not touch them!' God honors a person who 'swears to his own hurt and does not change' (Psalm 15:4). When you give your word in the Name of God, keep it even when it costs you. And look at what Joshua did with Gibeon: he made them woodcutters and water-carriers for the altar of Yahweh. Centered near God's presence, the Gibeonites were integrated into worship, and centuries later during the rebuild of Jerusalem, Gibeonites faithfully helped Nehemiah repair the walls (Nehemiah 3:7)! God redeems even our blunders when we walk in covenant integrity."
    },
