    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Give Me This Mountain: Why Faith Never Retires",
      "reflection": "Look at eighty-five-year-old Caleb in Joshua 14. For forty-five years, he had waited for his promised inheritance while an entire generation died off in the desert. He had buried friends, endured heat, and fought battles. By every standard of human culture, Caleb had earned the right to step back, put his feet up, and ask Joshua for a gentle, fertile valley with pre-built houses and pleasant vineyards. He could have said: 'Joshua, I'm eighty-five. Give me a quiet retirement plot.'\n\nInstead, Caleb stood tall, looked Joshua in the eye, and uttered five immortal words: **'GIVE ME THIS MOUNTAIN!'** (v. 12). And what mountain did he want? **Hebron!** Hebron was not a peaceful meadow; Hebron was the most rugged, high-altitude, rocky fortress in southern Canaan. And who lived on that mountain? The **Anakim**—the colossal, terrifying giants who had panicked the ten spies forty-five years earlier! At eighty-five years old, Caleb wanted to fight the giants!\n\nNotice Caleb's testimony in verse 11: 'I am as strong today as I was on the day Moses sent me!' Where did that supernatural vitality come from? It came from four words repeated three times in this passage: **'he wholly followed Yahweh'** (vv. 8, 9, 14). When your heart wholly follows God, your spiritual strength does not decay with age. The world tells older believers to step aside, disengage, and coast into comfortable obscurity. But in the kingdom of God, **faith never retires!** As long as there is breath in your lungs, God has mountains for you to conquer, giants for you to drive out, and younger generations like Othniel and Achsah for you to bless with upper and lower springs of living water. Stop looking for an easy valley; ask God for your mountain!"
    },
