    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "God Is Not Mocked: The Fire of Treachery Always Consumes the Bramble",
      "reflection": "Abimelech represents the toxic nightmare of self-appointed leadership. His story in Judges 9 is an unvarnished case study in raw political machination. He did not wait for God's call; he had no prophetic commission; he possessed no character. He weaponized tribal identity ('I am your bone and flesh'), bribed violent thugs with silver looted from an idol's temple, and systematically executed his seventy half-brothers on one rock to eliminate all political competition.\n\nFrom the top of Mount Gerizim, Jotham exposed the moral bankrupt nature of the regime through the Parable of the Trees. Productive, fruitful trees like the olive, fig, and vine have no interest in vain political domination—they are busy blessing God and man. Only the useless, prickly **bramble**, which offers no real shade and catches fire easily, covets despotic rule. And Jotham prophesied: the bramble will catch fire, and the fire will burn both the king and the citizens who crowned him.\n\nThree years later, the immutable law of the harvest took effect: 'Whatever a man sows, that he will also reap.' God sent an evil spirit of division. Treachery met treachery. And in the final stroke of divine poetic justice, the tyrant who slaughtered seventy brothers with heavy stones had his own skull crushed by an anonymous woman dropping an upper millstone from a tower roof. God sees every act of oppression, and no unrepentant tyrant ever escapes His righteous ledger."
    },
