    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Toying with Compromise Blinds Your Eyes Before It Breaks Your Chains",
      "reflection": "Judges 16 contains what is perhaps the most chilling verse in all of biblical narrative: 'He awoke out of his sleep, and said, \"I will go out as at other times, and shake myself free.\" But he did not know that Yahweh had departed from him.'\n\nSamson had fallen into the deadly trap of spiritual presumption. Because God had shown up so many times despite his recklessness—empowering him to slay a lion, snap cords, and carry away city gates—Samson assumed God's anointing was an entitlement. He thought he could flirt with Delilah, sleep in the camp of the enemy, gamble with his holy consecration, and still possess supernatural power whenever he chose.\n\nNotice the progression: first, Samson became spiritually blind through sensual infatuation; then he was physically blinded by his enemies. First, he was enslaved to his unchecked passions; then he was bound in bronze chains grinding wheat like an ox. Sin always takes you further than you wanted to go, keeps you longer than you wanted to stay, and costs you far more than you wanted to pay. Yet even in the pitch-black dungeon of Gaza, God's grace was quietly working: 'However, the hair of his head began to grow again.' God never completely abandons a broken heart."
    },
