    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "No Pit of Failure Is Too Deep for God's Restoring Grace",
      "reflection": "The final scene of Samson's life is a masterclass in divine redemption amidst tragic wreckage. Picture the scene: the pagan temple of Dagon is packed to capacity with thousands of Philistines chanting the praise of their fish-idol. In the center stands Samson—blind, scarred, stripped of his pride, led by the hand of a little servant boy, the laughingstock of the nation. In human eyes, Samson was a ruined, disqualified wreck whose story was over.\n\nYet inside that broken shell, something had transformed. The cocky, boastful hero who used to strut across the hills carrying city gates had finally learned to pray. Notice his words: 'Lord Yahweh, remember me, please, and strengthen me, please, only this once, God.' The man who once relied completely on his own muscles now placed his entire existence into the hands of Yahweh.\n\nGod heard that prayer. He did not reject Samson because of his catastrophic failures in Sorek. When Samson bowed with all his might, the pillars gave way, crushing the high command of Philistine idolatry. Hebrews 11 includes Samson in the Hall of Faith because 'from weakness he was made strong.' If you are sitting in the rubble of past failures, feeling that your compromises have permanently sidelined you, remember Samson. As long as you have breath in your lungs, you can turn your face to heaven, cry out to God, and watch His mercy triumph over failure."
    },
