    "whatGodIsSaying": {
      "headline": "Beware of 'Do-It-Yourself' Faith That Fashion God into Your Own Image",
      "reflection": "Judges 17 and 18 pull back the curtain on the spiritual rot of Israel when God's objective Word is abandoned. Micah's story sounds almost like dark satire: he steals 1,100 pieces of silver from his mother; when he confesses, she doesn't rebuke him—she dedicates the stolen silver to Yahweh to make a forbidden idol! Micah sets up a shrine, invents his own liturgy, and hires a nomadic Levite who cares more about a salary, free clothes, and meals than God's law.\n\nNotice Micah's tragic delusion: 'Now I know that Yahweh will do me good, because I have a Levite as my priest!' Micah treated religion as an insurance policy. He created a god he could control, an idol he could keep in his room, and a priest on his payroll.\n\nThen the Danites come along, muscle their way into the house, steal the idols, buy off the mercenary priest with a career promotion, and burn a peaceful town. When Micah chases after them, he cries the saddest line in the book: 'You have taken away my gods which I made... and what more do I have?' If your 'god' can be carved by a craftsman, kept in a cupboard, and carried off by thieves, you do not have God—you have an illusion. When everyone does what is right in their own eyes, true faith dissolves into self-serving consumerism."
    },
